[38;5;231m⏺[39m I've sketched the placement options in a browser tab. It should have opened on its own. If it didn't, here's the URL:

  [94m]8;id=125mv4g;http://localhost:51339/?key=36803021d1302e97508c59bc77d5b0a291258cd6a6611f1f73cbdda2e7fdbb25\http://localhost:51339/?key=36803021d1302e97508c59bc77d5b0a291258cd6a6611f1f73cbdda2e7fdbb25[39m]8;;\

  The browser view uses a lot of tokens, so tell me if you'd rather keep this in the terminal.

  There are three wireframes of the filter controls on the account activity page:

  - [1mA. Filter bar above the table.[0m Labeled controls for event type, device and date range sit in one row, always
    visible. There's a result count ("Showing 8 of 48 events") and a "Clear filters" button. The row wraps into stacked
    fields on narrow screens.
  - [1mB. One-click chips plus a smaller bar.[0m Chips cover the common cases, including "Failed sign-ins", which is what
    Security and the support tickets care about. Device and date range sit in a bar below the chips. The chip groupings
    are only examples; there are still exactly the 9 event types.
  - [1mC. A "Filter" button that opens a popover.[0m This keeps the header compact. Active filters show as removable tags next
    to the button.

  I'd go with A. It's the simplest to build and the most accessible. The labels stay visible, and there's no popover
  focus handling to get right. Filtering by "Sign-in failed" is one choice in the event type dropdown.

  The wireframes only show layout. I haven't picked which filters to include yet; that's my next question.

  Take a look and tell me what you think here in the terminal. You can also click an option in the browser to select it.

  Also, the companion saves its files under [38;5;153m.hyperpowers/[39m in the project root, and [38;5;153m.gitignore[39m doesn't list that folder
  yet. You may want to add it so the files don't end up in a commit.

